module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.arrow_domain_meaning_is_not_carried_on_the_node

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data arrow_domain_meaning_is_not_carried_on_the_node: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "arrow_domain_meaning_is_not_carried_on_the_node" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**an Arrow's first positional child means different things in different producers -- a parameter list of Named binders, one positional argument TYPE, or one record-typed payload -- and the Node does not say which, so every reader guesses from SHAPE, and a producer or fixture that builds the other meaning gets a reader's answer for the wrong one** (THE INVALID STATE is a well-formed Node whose domain a reader interprets under a meaning its producer did not intend. Readers read `first(children)` or the first positional child and treat a Named Conj there as a parameter list: `v2.std.node` `arrow_domain_binder_labels`, the resolve and symbol-index Arrow arms, infer's formal readers, and `v2.compiler.translate` `type_expr_arrow_split_from_types`, which reads 'every positional but the last' as inputs.)",

    "THE HARM is a wrong answer or a binding-time refusal at a reader far from the producer that caused it, and it is reached only when some claim happens to exercise that reader. Four receipts: (1) gunbc#12361 gave an Arrow a required signature-order edge; hand-built fixtures that did not know it were refused at binding time (`application_parameter_order_absent`) and ten claims went false, all outside the required floor's executed population (that population half is the landed row [[green_floor_executes_no_claim_whose_verdict_a_body_change_can_move]], not this one). (2) Service operations lowered their input payload as a Named Conj in domain position, so resolve bound the payload's field names as scope locals and `symbol_index_fill` indexed them at the operation. (3) `symbol_index_fill` consulted `v2.extdeps.languages.dag` `namespace_graft_spine_segment_edge_optional`, which reads ANY Conj with one Named edge to a Conj as a dotted-name spine segment, so a one-record-parameter domain lost its parameter level (repaired for Arrow domains in gunbc#12501; the shape-based recognizer was then deleted in gunbc#12611, and `namespace_graft_spine_body_optional` recognizes a segment only when its chain ends at the graft's body mark). (4) Resolve and symbol_index_fill read `first(children)` rather than the first positional child, so a Named edge placed first would be read as the domain (gunbc#12499, subsumed by #12501, with a discriminating red executed on main).",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS. Two Arrow ENCODINGS coexist, and that is a DESIGN section 3 fork in its own right: E1, the declared signature (`v2.std.compilers.body_lowering` `declared_signature`: a Named-binder domain plus the signature-order edge), and E2, a positional-type Arrow `[T1 .. Tn, U]` (`v2.std.algebra` `algebra_binary_fn_node`, the emitted add-fn Arrow in 14 language models, fn-type lowering). A third meaning, the operation payload, is resolved by making the payload ONE positional argument of record type (Y-wrap, gunbc#12501). The order edge is NOT derivable from the domain: `v2.std.node` `kind_edge_discipline` never reads the parent and content hashing sorts a Named Conj, so deleting the edge would make `f(a,b)` and `f(b,a)` one identity. A construction seal through `declared_signature` was considered and REFUSED: the Arrow kind reaches Node through generic builders a literal seal never sees, and sealing the value refuses every recognizer.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: 1. Each meaning conflict surfaced as a wrong answer or a binding-time refusal inside some claim, and only when a claim reached the reader. CEILING 3, structurally guaranteed: once one encoding holds, whether a Named-binder domain lacks its order edge is decidable from the Node alone (a domain with no Named binder is sound without one, since its order is positional and hashing keeps it), so well-formedness can refuse the bad state wherever a Node is admitted.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY: A SINGLE ARROW ENCODING -- E1 is the only Arrow encoding, sufficient for EVERY domain reader to read a Named-binder domain as a parameter list and nothing else. It is Program P (neat-boar-16's lane; its read-only census: 22 E2 producer sites in 16 files, 26 readers of which one, the translate split, needs a real rewrite onto `arrow_declared_parameter_order`, and no hash-keyed artifact). Once it holds, the climb is `v2.std.node` well-formedness refusing an Arrow whose domain carries a Named binder and no signature-order edge, on every path a Node is admitted (`v2.compiler.infer` `infer`, `assemble_program_from_ingest` and resolve included), with a discriminating RED refused there, a positive control of a positional no-binder Arrow admitted, the binding-time refusal kept as its own enrolled path, and `dpo_positional_binding_refuses_an_arrow_without_order` flipped and still enrolled (a stated reversal of #12361's 'an order-less Arrow is well formed').",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.std.arrow_signature", decl_name: "declared_signature", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.std.node", decl_name: "arrow_signature_order_conforms", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.std.node", decl_name: "arrow_domain_binder_labels", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.std.algebra", decl_name: "algebra_binary_fn_node", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.compiler.target_serialize", decl_name: "type_expr_arrow_split_from_types", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.extdeps.languages.dag", decl_name: "namespace_graft_spine_body_optional", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
